class:: SpecBandRatio
summary:: Fraction of energy within a frequency band
categories:: UGens>Analysis>MIR
related:: Guides/MIR2, Classes/SpecCentroid2

description::
Returns sum(w in selected range) / sum(w over the complete one-sided spectrum), in 0..1. Uniform level scaling cancels.

For these formulas w is the raw FFT magnitude or its square, and f is the bin-center frequency in Hz. Silence returns 0. The reader preserves the FFT chain. Peeters (2004), A Large Set of Audio Features for Sound Description; mathematical definitions are implemented independently. Source: link::https://recherche.ircam.fr/anasyn/peeters/ARTICLES/Peeters_2003_cuidadoaudiofeatures.pdf::.

CPU cost is linear in the number of FFT bins; no memory is allocated while processing. It adds no lookahead beyond the upstream FFT. At a 1024-sample window and 48 kHz the initial analysis window is about 21.3 ms. Changing FFT size or window affects unnormalized values.

Like vanilla link::Classes/SpecCentroid::, this reads FFT frames and produces control-rate timbre information; this class measures fraction of energy within a frequency band instead of the average frequency.

classmethods::
method:: kr

argument:: chain
FFT chain from an FFT with the same literal fftSize. New-frame values update the output; between frames the previous output is retained.

argument:: minFreq
Lowest included bin center in Hz; default 0 includes DC.

argument:: maxFreq
Highest included bin center in Hz; 0 selects Nyquist. Empty ranges return 0.

argument:: spectrumMode
Literal 0 uses magnitude; literal 1 uses squared magnitude (power).

argument:: fftSize
Literal power of two from 256 through 8192. Must match the FFT buffer.

returns::
One control-rate descriptor value.

examples::
SpecBandRatio calculates the ratio of energy in a specified frequency band to total spectral energy (between 0 and 1). In this example, it measures the proportion of energy in the 40 to 300 Hz low band. When low bass energy dominates (e.g. heavy kicks), an active mix tilt filter gently boosts high frequencies to maintain crisp clarity. An interactive code::MIR2GUI.scope:: displays real-time low-band energy ratio.
code::
(
s.waitForBoot({
    var path = MIR2GUI.soundPath("drums.wav");
    ~mir2Buffer = if(path.notNil) { Buffer.read(s, path) } { nil };
    ~mir2Bus = Bus.control(s, 1);
    s.sync;
    ~mir2Example = {
        var source = if(~mir2Buffer.notNil) {
            PlayBuf.ar(1, ~mir2Buffer, BufRateScale.kr(~mir2Buffer), loop: 1)
        } {
            // Synthetic drum fallback
            var trig = Impulse.kr(2);
            var kick = SinOsc.ar(EnvGen.ar(Env([120, 45], [0.08], \exp), trig)) * EnvGen.ar(Env.perc(0.005, 0.3), trig);
            var snare = WhiteNoise.ar * EnvGen.ar(Env.perc(0.005, 0.15), Impulse.kr(2, 0.5));
            (kick + snare) * 0.5
        };
        // For live input instead: source = SoundIn.ar(0);

        var chain = FFT(LocalBuf(1024), source, 0.25);
        var ratio = SpecBandRatio.kr(chain, minFreq: 40, maxFreq: 300, spectrumMode: 1, fftSize: 1024);
        var highBoost = ratio.lag(0.05).linlin(0.2, 0.8, 0.0, 6.0).clip(0.0, 8.0);
        var eq = BHiShelf.ar(source, 4000, 1.0, highBoost);
        var sound = eq * 0.25 ! 2;

        if(~mir2Bus.notNil) { Out.kr(~mir2Bus, ratio.clip(0, 1)) };
        sound * Line.kr(0, 1, 0.2)
    }.play;

    ~mir2GUI = MIR2GUI.scope("Low Band Energy Ratio", ~mir2Bus, 0.0, 1.0, "ratio");
});
)
::
code::
(
~mir2Example.free;
if(~mir2GUI.notNil) { ~mir2GUI.close };
if(~mir2Bus.notNil) { ~mir2Bus.free };
if(~mir2Buffer.notNil) { ~mir2Buffer.free };
)
::

